# Delegate Tasks to Subagents
 
 ## Core Principle
 
 The main thread must stay lean and sharp. Any task that consumes significant context (research, log analysis, debugging, file exploration, test execution, dependency installation, etc.) *must* be delegated to a subagent.
 
 ## When to Delegate
 
 Delegate to a subagent whenever the task involves:
 
 - Analyzing or exploring a tagged folder or large set of files
 - Running and interpreting test suites (npm test, jest, etc.)
 - Running npm install or other long-running shell commands
 - Reading logs, debugging errors, or investigating failures
 - Researching how something works across multiple files
 - Code review or auditing across a directory
 - Any operation expected to produce large output or require reading many files
 
 ## How to Delegate
 
 ### 1. Check existing specialized subagents first
 
 Before spawning a generic subagent, review the available subagent_type options and pick the best fit:
 
- explore — codebase exploration, file search, understanding structure
- shell — running commands (tests, installs, builds, git ops)
- librarian — monorepo catalog: where packages/domains/services live, what they do, who uses them; map feature descriptions to existing packages or gaps
- code-reviewer — expert code review for quality, security, and maintainability
- architect — transforms PRDs into implementation plans, maximizing code reuse
- business-analyst — product discovery, requirements gathering, and research; hands off to product-manager when done
- coder — writes production code from architect designs and test requirements
- documentation-writer — maintains package READMEs after code changes
- product-manager — translates feature descriptions into PRDs for engineers and QA
- project-manager — orchestrates the full implementation workflow across all agents
- tdd-unit-test-writer — writes failing tests first before implementation
- ui-designer — design systems, visual consistency, and user-centered interfaces
 
 ### 2. Fall back to generalPurpose when no specialist fits
 
 If none of the specialized subagents match the task, use subagent_type="generalPurpose".
 
 ### 3. Provide full context in the prompt
 
 Subagents do not inherit conversation history. Always include:
 
 - Exact file paths or directories to work with
 - What to look for or what to do
 - What information to return in the final response
 
 ## Examples
 
 
 # ❌ BAD — reading 20 files on main thread to understand a feature
 Read file1.ts → Read file2.ts → Read file3.ts → ... → summarize
 
 # ✅ GOOD — delegate exploration to a subagent
 Task(subagent_type="explore", prompt="Explore src/screens/product-lists-overview/ and explain how the analytics feature works. Return a summary of key files, data flow, and state management.")

 # ✅ GOOD — delegate catalog / reuse discovery to librarian
 Task(subagent_type="librarian", prompt="Feature: <description>. Return existing packages/domains/services that could cover it (paths, package names, role, consumers), gaps, and suggested placement if nothing fits. Use pnpm-workspace.yaml and READMEs as evidence.")
 
 
 
 # ❌ BAD — running tests and reading output on main thread
 Shell("npm test -- --coverage") → read large output → analyze
 
 # ✅ GOOD — delegate to shell subagent
 Task(subagent_type="shell", prompt="Run `npm test -- --coverage` in services/xsite-cms-bos/ and report: pass/fail count, any failures with file + error, and coverage summary.")
 
 
 
 # ❌ BAD — npm install on main thread consuming scrollback
 Shell("npm install")
 
 # ✅ GOOD — delegate to shell subagent
 Task(subagent_type="shell", prompt="Run `npm install` in the repo root. Report success or any errors/warnings.")
 
 
 ## What Stays on the Main Thread
 
 - Short, focused edits to 1-3 files
 - Single file reads when you already know the exact path
 - Quick grep/glob lookups for a specific symbol
 - Responding to the user with summaries and decisions
 - Coordinating and dispatching subagents